From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Austin Schuh <austin.linux@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:31:10 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] sched/rt: Push the running task away from a pinned task again

Commit dae5c0292080 ("sched/rt,dl: Skip migrate-disabled tasks when
picking a push candidate") made pick_next_pushable_task() skip
migrate-disabled tasks.  push_rt_task() used it to find the task to
push, so it no longer sees a migrate-disabled task waiting behind
rq->curr, and never takes the fallback that pushes rq->curr to a
lower-priority CPU instead.  The pinned task now waits until rq->curr
sleeps, even with other CPUs idle.

The commit's message says nothing is lost because that fallback always
failed the re-check in find_lock_lowest_rq().  That holds when the
pinned task and rq->curr have the same priority, as in its trace.  When
rq->curr has higher priority, the usual case since it preempted the
pinned task, rq->curr heads the pushable list once the stopper preempts
it, the re-check passes, and the push works.

On PREEMPT_RT every task in a spinlock_t section is migrate-disabled, so
this is common.  On a Jetson Orin (6.18.59-rt6), a FIFO 5 task preempted
in one by a FIFO 6 CPU hog waited up to 133 ms with two of the six CPUs
idle.  On 6.18.39, without the commit, the same test never waited more
than 10 ms.

Look for a pinned task at the head of the queue first, as before.  With
pick_next_pushable_task() now skipping pinned tasks, the re-check passes
for rq->curr in the equal-priority case too, so the push-IPI loop the
commit fixed doesn't come back: rq->curr moves away and the pinned task
runs.

Fixes: dae5c0292080 ("sched/rt,dl: Skip migrate-disabled tasks when picking a push candidate")
Upstream-Status: Pending
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 kernel/sched/rt.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/kernel/sched/rt.c b/kernel/sched/rt.c
index fc9737decfb7..2942a7c638b1 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/rt.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/rt.c
@@ -1851,6 +1851,29 @@ static int find_lowest_rq(struct task_struct *task)
 	return -1;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The highest priority queued task, if it is migrate-disabled.  On PREEMPT_RT
+ * that's common: any task preempted inside a spinlock_t section.  It can't be
+ * pushed, so push_rt_task() pushes the running task away instead, which gives
+ * the pinned task its CPU back.  pick_next_pushable_task() skips such a task,
+ * so it can't find it.
+ */
+static struct task_struct *pick_next_pinned_task(struct rq *rq)
+{
+	struct task_struct *p;
+
+	if (!has_pushable_tasks(rq))
+		return NULL;
+
+	plist_for_each_entry(p, &rq->rt.pushable_tasks, pushable_tasks) {
+		if (task_on_cpu(rq, p))
+			continue;
+		return is_migration_disabled(p) ? p : NULL;
+	}
+
+	return NULL;
+}
+
 static struct task_struct *pick_next_pushable_task(struct rq *rq)
 {
 	struct plist_head *head = &rq->rt.pushable_tasks;
@@ -1954,7 +1977,9 @@ static int push_rt_task(struct rq *rq, bool pull)
 	if (!rq->rt.overloaded)
 		return 0;
 
-	next_task = pick_next_pushable_task(rq);
+	next_task = pick_next_pinned_task(rq);
+	if (!next_task)
+		next_task = pick_next_pushable_task(rq);
 	if (!next_task)
 		return 0;
 
